\description{
Wraps \code{\link[sensemakr]{sensemakr}} (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020) in a
bootstrap loop. For an omitted variable \code{kd} times as strong as an
observed benchmark covariate, \code{bootmakr()} computes the bias-adjusted
estimate of the treatment coefficient and obtains its standard error,
confidence interval and p-value by simple, stratified or cluster bootstrap,
so that inference does not rest on the conventional OLS variance formula.
}
\details{
In every replication the data are resampled (rows, whole clusters, or
within strata), the regression is refitted, and the benchmark bounds and
the bias-adjusted estimate are recomputed. The bootstrap distribution
therefore reflects sampling uncertainty in the regression coefficient
\emph{and} in the estimated strength of the benchmark. The standard error
is the standard deviation of that distribution, the confidence interval
its percentile interval, and the p-value is twice the smaller of the
shares of replications at or below zero and at or above zero.

With a length-two \code{cluster}, three bootstraps are combined with the
Cameron, Gelbach & Miller (2011) subtractive variance estimator, and the
confidence interval and p-value use a normal approximation.

Below the table of adjusted estimates, the print method reports the
\strong{benchmark strength}: the partial R2 of the benchmark with the
treatment (given the other covariates) and with the outcome (given the
treatment and the covariates), and sensemakr's implied bound on the
omitted variable at each \code{kd}. For the omitted variable it also
reports its \strong{impact}, the product of its partial correlations with
the outcome and with the treatment, both given the covariates only. This
is the scale on which the impact threshold of a confounding variable
(ITCV) is stated, so a reader used to the ITCV can judge how strong the
assumed omitted variable is. The partial correlation with the outcome
given the covariates is recovered from sensemakr's bound (which conditions
on the treatment as well) with the recursion formula for partial
correlations, with the signs set so that the omitted variable biases the
estimate away from zero (towards zero when \code{reduce = FALSE}). All of
these are functions of the data alone. No standard error enters them, so
they do not depend on the variance estimator, and none of them is a
threshold. They are stored in \code{$benchmark_strength}.

The print method then reports the \strong{breakdown kd}: the smallest
\code{kd}, with \code{ky = kd}, at which the estimate is no longer significant
at \code{alpha}, for the benchmark the table of adjusted estimates refers to.
Two numbers are given. The first is judged by the confidence interval that
\code{sensemakr::ovb_bounds()} reports, which rests on the conventional OLS
standard error. The second is judged by the bootstrap test of the table:
running \code{bootmakr()} with the same \code{seed} and a \code{kd} marginally
below that value returns a p-value below \code{alpha}, and marginally above it
a p-value of at least \code{alpha}. It is computed without refitting, from the
coefficient, its scale and the benchmark's two partial R2 in every replicate
(\code{$boot_base} and \code{$boot_bench}), and it does not depend on the
\code{kd} of the call. sensemakr's bound exists only up to a largest
\code{kd}, at which the implied partial R2 of the omitted variable with the
treatment reaches one. That limit is printed. It differs between replicates,
and a replicate beyond its own limit is left out, as it is in the table; a
note says how many are left out at the reported value. A benchmark that is
strongly related to the treatment has a low limit, and close to the limit the
bias grows without bound, so a breakdown kd near the limit reflects the limit
more than the estimate. With \code{weights} both numbers use the weighted
partial R2 of the benchmark, and with \code{semiweights} the bound of
Wainstein and Hazlett (2026), whose limit is \code{(1 - R2w) / R2s}. The
breakdown kd is available for \code{reduce = TRUE} and when the table reports
a benchmark rather than a manual R2. It is stored in \code{$breakdown}.

The print method also reports the \strong{robustness value} of the
treatment coefficient, which needs no benchmark: the strength an omitted
variable must have, as a partial R2 with the treatment and with the
outcome alike, to overturn the result. Three numbers are given. The
first two are \code{sensemakr}'s: the strength that brings the adjusted
estimate to zero, and the strength at which the adjusted estimate is no
longer significant at \code{alpha} when judged with the conventional OLS
standard error. The third is the bootstrap counterpart of the second:
the smallest strength at which the bootstrap test used for the table of
adjusted estimates stops rejecting.
Running \code{bootmakr()} with the same \code{seed} and with \code{r2dz.x} and
\code{r2yz.dx} both set marginally above that value returns a p-value of at
least \code{alpha}; marginally below it, a p-value below \code{alpha}. As in
the table, the omitted variable is taken to pull the coefficient of each
replicate towards zero, whatever its sign in that replicate. When many
replicates have a coefficient of the opposite sign to the estimate, as with a
weak effect, this convention raises the bootstrap value, and a bootstrap value
above \code{sensemakr}'s should then not be read as greater robustness.
It is
computed from the coefficient and the residual scale of every replicate,
without refitting, uses every replicate in which the regression could be
fitted, and is available for \code{q = 1} and \code{reduce = TRUE}. It
carries the same notion of uncertainty as the table: the sampling
variability of the adjusted estimate for an omitted variable of given
strength. It is not a statement about the robust standard error that the
regression including the omitted variable would have had. The three
numbers are stored in \code{$robustness}.

The breakdown kd and the bootstrap robustness value are set by the tail of the
bootstrap distribution: with \code{alpha = 0.05}, by the 2.5 percent of
replicates closest to zero. They therefore need more replications than a
standard error does. Use 1,000 or more, and expect them still to differ by a
few percent from one seed to the next. The sign convention described for the
robustness value applies to the breakdown kd as well, so with a weak effect
the bootstrap value can exceed the conventional one for that reason alone.

After the robustness values the print method sets the \strong{standard errors
of the adjusted estimate} side by side, one row per \code{kd}. The first is
the bootstrap standard error of the table. The second, shown when a benchmark
is re-estimated in every replicate, is the bootstrap standard error with the
strength of the omitted variable held at its value in the original fit. It is
computed from the same replicates, and the difference between the two is the
part of the uncertainty that comes from estimating the benchmark. The third is
\code{sensemakr}'s adjusted standard error, which rests on the conventional
OLS standard error. The fourth is the closed form of Wainstein and Hazlett
(2026): the robust standard error of the original fit multiplied by
\code{sqrt((1 - r2yz.dx) / (1 - r2dz.x))}, with one more regressor in its
finite-sample correction. The robust standard error is HC1 without clusters,
CR1 with one cluster variable and, as an extension made here, the Cameron,
Gelbach and Miller combination of CR1 variances with two; strata are ignored.
The finite-sample corrections of the variance are \code{n / (n - k)} for HC1
and \code{G / (G - 1) * (n - 1) / (n - k)} for CR1, with \code{n} the number
of observations with a positive weight, \code{G} the number of clusters and
\code{k} the number of regressors, which for the adjusted standard error
includes the omitted variable (equation 58 of Wainstein and Hazlett, 2026).
The closed form assumes that the omitted variable shrinks every residual of
the outcome and of the treatment by the same proportion, and Acedanski (2026)
shows that an omitted variable can move a robust standard error by much more
than that. The last two standard errors refer to the regression that would
include the omitted variable. The bootstrap refers to the sampling variability
of the adjusted estimate, so the columns answer different questions and need
not agree. They are stored in \code{$se_compare}.

The print method ends with the \strong{ratio of the robust to the conventional
standard error} of the treatment coefficient in the unadjusted regression, and
with the change in that ratio when each benchmark covariate is taken out of
the regression and put back (Acedanski, 2026, Section 6.2). A ratio away from
one means that robust and conventional inference already differ before any
omitted variable is considered. The change is a yardstick for how far one more
covariate might move the ratio. Both are stored in \code{$se_ratio}, whose
\code{cap} is the ratio plus the largest positive change.

The bootstrap procedure follows Cinelli, Ferwerda and Hazlett (2024,
Appendix C); Lonati and Wulff (2026) show why it is needed when the
regression calls for robust standard errors.
}
\examples{
# Simulated firm panel: the true effect of x on y is 0.25, and a firm-level
# variable q, omitted here, biases the regression of y on x and c (see
# ?firms). An omitted variable as strong as the observed control c (kd = 1,
# the default); firms are resampled as whole clusters. 200 replications
# keep the example quick; use 1,000 or more in practice.
data("firms", package = "bootmakr")
out <- bootmakr(y ~ x + c, data = firms, treat = "x",
                benchmark_covariates = "c", cluster = "firm",
                reps = 200, seed = 1, progress = FALSE)
out

# The strength of the benchmark and of the omitted variable it implies,
# with the impact of the latter on the scale of the ITCV
out$benchmark_strength

# Several strengths at once: where does the effect stop being significant?
sweep <- bootmakr(y ~ x + c, data = firms, treat = "x",
                  benchmark_covariates = "c", kd = c(0.5, 1, 1.5, 2),
                  cluster = "firm", reps = 200, seed = 1, progress = FALSE)
sweep$results
plot(sweep, type = "kd_sweep")

}
